  "helpProbableQ": "What does \"most likely\" mean?",
  "helpProbableA": "We looked at how the rain came in your valley in years like this one. \"Most likely\" is what happened most often. It isn't certain: that's why I also show you the other possibilities.",
  "helpProbableK": "likely,sure,certain,percent,chance,probability",
  "helpSourceQ": "Where does the forecast come from?",
  "helpSourceA": "This week's rain comes from the daily forecast. For the coming months we use NASA satellites: rain (GPM), heat (NASA POWER) and soil moisture (SMAP) in your valley. We also check ENFEN's bulletins about El Niño.",
  "helpSourceK": "nasa,satellite,source,forecast,data,enfen,nino",
  "helpRainMmQ": "What are mm of rain?",
  "helpRainMmA": "It's how much water falls. 1 mm is like a layer of water 1 millimeter deep over your field. 40 mm in one day is already heavy rain: better not to fertilize or spray that day.",
  "helpRainMmK": "mm,millimeter,how much rain",
  "helpMoistureQ": "How does the satellite know if my soil is wet?",
  "helpMoistureA": "NASA's SMAP satellite measures soil moisture from space every few days. It measures the whole area, so it may differ a little from what you see in your field.",
  "helpMoistureK": "moisture,wet,dry,smap,humidity",
  "helpHarvestQ": "What is the expected harvest?",
  "helpHarvestA": "It's how much you can expect to harvest compared with a good season of that crop in your valley. 100% would be a full harvest. If it's less, it's because the weather or the soil may take some away. We estimate it from the greenness satellites saw in similar years.",
  "helpHarvestK": "harvest,yield,production,expected",
  "helpWaterQ": "What does the water mean?",
  "helpWaterA": "It's the water the crops need in a season, per hectare. It's measured in cubic meters: 1 m³ is like a 1,000-liter tank. Using less water helps when the reservoir is low.",
  "helpWaterK": "water,irrigation,m3,m³,cubic",
  "helpSoilQ": "What does \"your soil: good\" mean?",
  "helpSoilA": "It's how your soil would end up after 2 years. If you plant the same thing again and again, the soil gets tired and more pests come. If you change crops and add beans or pulses, the soil rests and gets better.",
  "helpSoilK": "soil,land,tired,good,fair",
  "helpDeadlineQ": "Why do I have to decide before a date?",
  "helpDeadlineA": "Because you need to prepare the soil and get the seed before planting starts. If you wait longer, you may miss the best time for that crop.",
  "helpDeadlineK": "date,when,decide,december,before,deadline",
  "helpBlocksQ": "How do I read the colored blocks?",
  "helpBlocksA": "Each block is a season. Read from top to bottom: the first is what you plant now and the last is 2 years from now. Each crop has its own color, so you can quickly see if you repeat the same one.",
  "helpBlocksK": "color,colors,block,read,plan",
  "helpRotateQ": "Why change crops?",
  "helpRotateA": "Each crop takes different things from the soil and attracts different pests. If you change, the soil recovers and pests don't build up. That way your harvest holds up better in hard years.",
  "helpRotateK": "rotation,rotate,change crops,pests,why change",
  "helpGreenManureQ": "What is green manure?",
  "helpGreenManureA": "It's a plant you sow but don't harvest: you leave it in the soil so it breaks down and feeds it. It's like giving your field rest and food at the same time.",
  "helpGreenManureK": "manure,green,cover crop,rest",
